
STACK MEMORY:
=============
  - It stores temporary variables and separate memory block for threads
  - Store primitive data types
  - Store reference of the Heap objects
        - Strong reference
        - Weak reference
            - Soft reference
  - Each thread has its own Stack memory
  - Variables within a SCOPE is only visible and as soon as any variable is out of SCOPE, it gets deleted
    from the Stack(in LIFO order).
  - When Stack memory goes full, it throws "java.lang.StackOverflowError"


HEAP MEMORY:
=============
  - It stores objects.
  - There is no order of allocating the memory.
  - Garbage Collector is used to delete  the unreferenced objects from the Heap.
        - Mark and Sweep Algorithm
        - Types of GC:
          - Single GC
          - Parallel GC
          - CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep)
          - G1
  - Heap Memory is stored with all the threads.
  - Heap Memory also contains the String Pool.
  - When Heap memory goes full, it throws "java.lang.OutOfMemoryError"
  - Heap Memory is futher divided into:
        - Young Generation (minor GC happens here)
          - Eden
          - Survivor
        - Old Generation (major GC happens here)
        - Permanent Generation (It was part of Heap memory and it was not extendable) old Java version)

          This is now replaced by Metaspace from JDK 8.0 (this is expandable since it is NOT part of HEAP memory)

